Dispatcher code that is the same in every process, and run from an AOT image (FEX 14c9268, on top of 0005)

iOS without JIT can only run code that is signed into the app, so FEX's
dispatcher (generated at startup) has to be built in advance like the AOT
caches. Its code was position independent except for four 64-bit literals:
the context and the addresses of SleepThread, CompileBlock and
CompileSingleStep. Those now live in the thread's JITPointers
(DispatcherCTX, DispatcherSleepThread, DispatcherCompileBlock,
DispatcherCompileSingleStep), filled by InitThreadPointers, and the
dispatcher loads them from there. Its code is then identical in every
process of the same build and configuration (except in the VIXL simulator,
whose indirect calls embed host addresses).

The DumpDispatcher option writes the generated code to a file; WinPad's
tools/cache2macho --dispatcher turns it into assembly with the symbols
fexaot_dispatcher_text and fexaot_dispatcher_meta. With AOTImage set, the
dispatcher is still generated, but into memory that is not executable; if
the image's copy is byte for byte the same, every entry point is moved to
it ("Dispatcher: static code from AOT image"), otherwise FEX makes its own
copy executable and uses that as before.

